<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belz is a small town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> Oblast in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, near the Polish border. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the Cherven towns disputed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Rus', the seat of a principality and from 1462 the capital of the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz-voivodeship">Belz Voivodeship</a>. From the early 19th century it was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz-hasidic-dynasty">Belz Hasidic dynasty</a>, and its large Jewish community was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Belz lies on a fertile plain near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, between the Solokiya River, a tributary of the Bug, and the Richytsia stream. In ancient times the plain was settled by peoples of Indo-European origin: the Celtic Lugii, later Germanic Goths, Slavized Sarmatians and finally the Slavic Dulebes, who became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> in 907, according to the usual account.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town has existed since at least the 10th century as one of the Cherven Cities, which were under Polish rule in the 970s. In 981 it was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, and in 1170 it became the seat of the Duchy of Belz. During the Wars of the Galician Succession the city and its surroundings were plundered by the army of Prince Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and his brother Vasylko Romanovych. From 1234 it belonged to the Duchy of Galicia–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, and from 1340 it came under Lithuanian rule.</p><p>Belz was under Polish rule from 1366 to 1772, first as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> and from 1462 as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz-voivodeship">Belz Voivodeship</a>. On 5 October 1377 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole">Władysław Opolczyk</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a>, granted the town <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights, which a charter of 10 November 1509 confirmed.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 brought it into the Habsburg Empire, where it lay in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, and a railway reached it in 1884. After 1918 it was briefly part of the Western Ukrainian People's Republic, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1919. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupied it from 1939 to 1944, and a border readjustment passed it from Poland to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1951.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Belz is a small town in Lviv Oblast with 2,191 inhabitants in a 2022 estimate, part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> since 1991. Its Jewish history is central to its fame. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> community was established around the 14th century, and Shalom Rokeach (1779–1855) became the first Belzer Rebbe in the early 19th century. At the start of the First World War Belz had 6,100 inhabitants, among them 3,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. On 2 June 1942 about 1,000 Jews were deported to Hrubieszów and from there to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobibor-extermination-camp">Sobibor extermination camp</a>.</p><p>The fourth Rebbe, Aharon Rokeach, escaped to Israel in 1944 and re-established the dynasty there. The town is also remembered as the place where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-madonna-of-czestochowa">Black Madonna of Częstochowa</a> resided until 1382, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole">Władysław Opolczyk</a> took it to his own principality.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Belz</b>, on a tributary of the Bug, was one of the Cherven towns contested between Poland and Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-belz">Principality of Belz</a></li></ul></section>
